#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# region_union

#% text
# `region_union(r1, r2, ...)` is the union of plane polygons (rectangles, triangles, and disks as fine polygons) as a polygon.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Several pieces give a polygon of several rings; holes give `polygon(outer -> holes)`.

#% item
# Coordinates are computed with machine numbers; exact input gives exact rationals back where they are small.

#% item
# Mesh regions give a mesh of the kind of the first: meshes of segments and boundary meshes of points on the line,
# boundary meshes of segments in the plane, boundary meshes of polygons in space (by binary space partitioning).

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# Two overlapping squares:

#% cell
region_union(rectangle([0, 0], [2, 2]), rectangle([1, 1], [3, 3]))
#% output
#= polygon([[0, 0], [2, 0], [2, 1], [3, 1], [3, 3], [1, 3], [1, 2], [0, 2]])

#% text
# Its area:

#% cell
area(region_union(rectangle([0, 0], [2, 2]), rectangle([1, 1], [3, 3])))
#% output
#= 7

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Meshes of the line give a mesh of the same kind:

#% cell
region_union(mesh_region([[0.], [2.]], [line([[1, 2]])]), mesh_region([[1.], [3.]], [line([[1, 2]])]))
#% output
#= mesh_region([[0.0], [3.0]], [line([[1, 2]])])

#% text
# Two tetrahedra given by their boundary faces: the boundary of the solid they fill together.

#% cell
t1 = boundary_mesh_region([[0., 0., 0.], [1., 0., 0.], [0., 1., 0.], [0., 0., 1.]], [polygon([[3, 2, 1], [1, 2, 4], [2, 3, 4], [4, 3, 1]])]);
t2 = boundary_mesh_region([[0., 0., 0.], [1., 0., 0.], [0., 1., 0.], [1., 1., 2.]], [polygon([[3, 2, 1], [1, 2, 4], [2, 3, 4], [4, 3, 1]])]);
volume(region_union(t1, t2))
#% output
#= 0.41666666666666663

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `region_intersection`, `region_difference`
